The '''2026 Sunderland City Council election''' was held on 7 May 2026, alongside the [[2026 United Kingdom local elections|other local elections across the United Kingdom]] which were held on the same day. All 75 members of [[Sunderland City Council]] were elected.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-01-01 |title=Everything you need to know about landmark Sunderland 2026 local elections |url=https://www.sunderlandecho.com/news/politics/council/everything-you-need-to-know-about-landmark-sunderland-2026-local-elections-that-will-reset-political-map-5459638 |access-date=2026-01-27 |website=Sunderland Echo |language=en}}</ref> Boundary changes were made for these elections.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-11-15 |title=Sunderland facing 'all-out' elections in 2026 with boundary changes expected for city |url=https://www.sunderlandecho.com/news/politics/council/sunderland-facing-all-out-elections-in-2026-with-boundary-changes-expected-for-city-3918915 |access-date=2026-01-27 |website=Sunderland Echo |language=en}}</ref> 

[[Reform UK]] took control of the council, wiping out the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]] and reducing [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]], who had ran the city for fifty years, to just 5 councillors. [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] held all 12 seats returning as the majority opposition party.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Manning |first=Jonny |date=2026-05-08 |title=Reform UK takes control of Sunderland City Council from Labour |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5yr661y6y4o |access-date=2026-05-08 |website=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref>

== Background ==
Before the election [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] was the largest party with [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] forming the official opposition after overtaking the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]] at the [[2023 Sunderland City Council election|2023]] and [[2024 Sunderland City Council election|2024]] elections.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-05-03 |title=Calderdale Council local election results: Labour retains control |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c0v05pd1qg4o |access-date=2026-01-27 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref> Since the last election two councillors defected from both Labour and the Conservatives to sit as [[Independent politician|Independents]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-06-25 |title=Councillor quits Labour over what he calls 'anti-democratic removal' of former leader |url=https://www.sunderlandecho.com/news/politics/council/sunderland-councillor-resigns-from-labour-party-over-what-he-calls-anti-democratic-removal-of-former-council-leader-4679120 |access-date=2026-03-21 |website=Sunderland Echo |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-06-17 |title=Conservative councillor resigns to sit as independent |url=https://www.sunderlandecho.com/news/politics/conservative-councillor-simon-ayre-resigns-to-sit-as-independent-5180969 |access-date=2026-03-21 |website=Sunderland Echo |language=en}}</ref>

In November 2025 [[Reform UK]] won their first seat on the council with a 35% swing in the Hetton ward by-election, however their councillor resigned from the party after just 76 days and now sits as an independent.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-02-13 |title=Reform UK label councillor's decision to quit the party a betrayal |url=https://www.sunderlandecho.com/news/people/reform-uk-label-councillors-decision-to-quit-the-party-a-betrayal-5597379 |access-date=2026-03-21 |website=Sunderland Echo |language=en}}</ref> There is speculation that Reform will take control of the council like it had the year before in [[2025 Durham County Council election|County Durham]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Holland |first=Daniel |date=2026-03-18 |title=Reform to win four more North East councils in May, new polling predicts |url=https://www.chroniclelive.co.uk/news/north-east-news/reform-set-win-control-four-33618455 |access-date=2026-03-22 |website=Chronicle Live |language=en}}</ref>

In March 2026, Fiona Miller a Labour councillor first elected in 2011 defected to the [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]]. Her husband Graeme Miller was council leader for Labour between 2018 and 2024 before he was removed by [[National Executive Committee of the Labour Party (UK)|Labour's National Executive Committee]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-05-21 |title=New Sunderland City Council leader begins first day at the job after official vote takes place |url=https://www.sunderlandecho.com/news/politics/council/new-sunderland-city-council-leader-begins-first-day-at-the-job-after-official-vote-takes-place-4636844 |access-date=2026-03-25 |website=Sunderland Echo |language=en}}</ref>

== Ward results ==
An asterisk next to a candidate's name denotes an incumbent councillor. Each ward will elect three candidates, the candidate with the highest number of votes will receive a four year term until 2030, the candidate with the second highest number of votes will receive a two year term until May 2028, and the candidate with the third highest number of votes will receive a one year term until May 2027.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Local Government Elections - Thursday 7 May 2026 |url=https://www.sunderland.gov.uk/article/38886/Local-Government-Elections-Thursday-7-May-2026 |access-date=2026-04-10 |website=Sunderland City Council |language=en}}</ref>

